    "question": "Jim reads at a rate of 40 pages an hour.  He reads a total of 600 pages per week.  He increases his reading speed to 150% of its former speed but reads 4 hours less per week.  How many pages does he read a week now?",
    "gold": "660",
    "answer": "He read 600/40=<<600/40=15>>15 hours per week\nSo now he reads 15-4=<<15-4=11>>11 hours per week\nShe now reads 40*1.5=<<40*1.5=60>>60 pages per hour\nSo she reads 11*60=<<11*60=660>>660 pages per week\n#### 660"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many pages Jim reads per week after increasing his reading speed, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate Jim's original reading speed and the number of pages he reads per week:**\n   - Jim's original reading speed is 40 pages per hour.\n   - He reads a total of 600 pages per week.\n   - The number of hours he reads per week is given by:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Hours per week} = \\frac{\\text{Total pages per week}}{\\text{Reading speed}} = \\frac{600}{40} = 15 \\text{ hours}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Determine Jim's new reading speed and the number of hours he reads per week after the increase:**\n   - Jim increases his reading speed to 150% of his original speed.\n   - 150% of 40 pages per hour is:\n     \\[\n     1.5 \\times 40 = 60 \\text{ pages per hour}\n     \\]\n   - He reads 4 hours less per week, so the new number of hours he reads per week is:\n     \\[\n     15 - 4 = 11 \\text{ hours}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total number of pages Jim reads per week with his new reading speed and hours:**\n   - The total number of pages he reads per week is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Total pages per week} = \\text{Reading speed} \\times \\text{Hours per week} = 60 \\times 11 = 660 \\text{ pages}\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of pages Jim reads per week now is \\(\\boxed{660}\\).",
